WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.267
Now we implement a small overlapping chunker.

00:00:03.267 --> 00:00:09.112
The function splits the text into words, takes a fixed number of words for each chunk,

00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:13.657
and then moves forward while keeping some overlap from the previous chunk.

00:00:13.657 --> 00:00:17.402
Focus on the logic rather than memorizing the code.

00:00:17.402 --> 00:00:20.877
The key variables are chunk size and overlap.

00:00:20.877 --> 00:00:29.562
If the chunk size is fifty-five words and the overlap is fifteen, the next chunk begins before the previous chunk

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:34.055
completely ends. That repeated text can help preserve meaning around the boundary.

00:00:34.742 --> 00:00:38.184
After you run the function, inspect the returned strings.

00:00:38.184 --> 00:00:44.189
Do they read naturally? Does each chunk contain enough information to be useful on its own?

00:00:44.189 --> 00:00:47.064
Do not rush directly to embeddings.

00:00:47.064 --> 00:00:51.284
The embedding model will happily create vectors for bad chunks.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:53.949
Human inspection is still valuable.

00:00:53.949 --> 00:01:00.256
Later, evaluation will help us decide whether a different chunk size actually improves retrieval.
